```cpp
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
int main() {
    int result = factorial(3);
    return 0;
}
```

Conceptual Call Stack (bottom to top):

```
[ main() stack frame ]
    - result variable
    - return address to OS/caller
[ factorial(3) stack frame ]
    - n = 3
    - return address to main()
[ factorial(2) stack frame ]
    - n = 2
    - return address to factorial(3)
[ factorial(1) stack frame ]
    - n = 1
    - return address to factorial(2)
```

At this point, `factorial(1)` has just received the return value from `factorial(0)` (which returned `1`) and is about to compute `1 * 1` and return to `factorial(2)`. The `factorial(0)` frame would have already been popped.
